    ],
    "text": "1 Abstract"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000025",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0002-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0002-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0002-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0002-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0002-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0002-l0003"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0002-l0000",
      "p0003-n0002-l0001",
      "p0003-n0002-l0002",
      "p0003-n0002-l0003"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "We present a user-friendly strategy for keeping the water in a bathtub as close as possible to a certain preferred temperature. This strategy consists in two main constituents: adjusting the hot water \ufb02ow from the faucet to keep the bathtub temperature suitably high, and using hand motions to distribute the heat evenly throughout the bath water."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000026",
    "issues": [
      "hyphen_join_review"
    ],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0003-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0003-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0003-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0003-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0003-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0003-l0003"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0003-l0003",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0003-l0004"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0003-l0004",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0003-l0005"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0003-l0000",
      "p0003-n0003-l0001",
      "p0003-n0003-l0002",
      "p0003-n0003-l0003",
      "p0003-n0003-l0004",
      "p0003-n0003-l0005"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "As for determining the necessary water \ufb02ow, we \ufb01rst create a model that determines the tem- perature of the bath water at any point in time. We posit that the temperature of the water in the bath is dependent on the cooling due to the ambient temperature of the room, which obeys the proportionality given by Newton\u2019s Law of Cooling; and the heating due to the added hot water from the faucet. Our model combines the temperature changes over time from these two entities into one consummate di\ufb00erential equation."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000027",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0004-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0004-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0004-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0004-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0004-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0004-l0003"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0004-l0003",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0004-l0004"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0004-l0004",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0004-l0005"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0004-l0000",
      "p0003-n0004-l0001",
      "p0003-n0004-l0002",
      "p0003-n0004-l0003",
      "p0003-n0004-l0004",
      "p0003-n0004-l0005"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "We determine the experimental constant of proportionality for Newton\u2019s Law of Cooling by proposing that this constant itself is dependent on a combination of volume of the water and surface area exposed to the air. Taking experimental data from varied sources, including an experiment we conducted and documented over the time frame of the MCM competition, we were able to extrapolate values for the constant attributable to any bathtub given its volume and surface area exposed to air."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000028",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0005-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0005-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0005-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0005-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0005-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0005-l0003"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0005-l0003",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0005-l0004"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0005-l0000",
      "p0003-n0005-l0001",
      "p0003-n0005-l0002",
      "p0003-n0005-l0003",
      "p0003-n0005-l0004"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "We then run a computer simulation to model the temperature changes over time in a standard bathtub with our di\ufb00erential equation, determining how much hot water needs to \ufb02ow in to keep the total deviance over time of the temperature of the bath from a desired temperature at a minimum. We found the optimal \ufb02ow rate for a standard bathtub at standard conditions to be 1.81 gallons/minute, or about 11% of the maximum \ufb02ow rate."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000029",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0006-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0006-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0006-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0006-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0006-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0006-l0003"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0006-l0000",
      "p0003-n0006-l0001",
      "p0003-n0006-l0002",
      "p0003-n0006-l0003"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "Our model was able to predict optimal \ufb02ow rates for many di\ufb00erent combinations of volume, surface area, tub material, and additives. We found that more e\ufb00ective insulating materials led to lower optimal \ufb02ow rates (less hot water needed), as did the addition of additives. Volume and surface area, surprisingly, showed no clear trend."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000030",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0007-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0007-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0007-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0007-l0002"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0007-l0000",
      "p0003-n0007-l0001",
      "p0003-n0007-l0002"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "As for keeping the temperature roughly even in the bathtub, we suggest that the user swirl his hands counter-clockwise with respect to the faucet as shown in Figure 1. This is a simple yet e\ufb00ective way to distribute the heat acceptably evenly throughout the tub water."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000031",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0003-n0008-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0003-n0008-l0001"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0004",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0003-n0008-l0000",
      "p0003-n0008-l0001"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      3
    ],
    "text": "As alternatives for those who do not wish to make their bath into an exercise session, we invented an original product (Home Jacuzzi) that is low-cost and aids in heating the water evenly."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000032",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "heading",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "operation": "same_baseline_heading_fragments",
        "source_line_ids": [
          "p0003-n0009-l0000",
